<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Tartu (Finnish–Russian)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1920</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Tartu was signed on 14 October 1920 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, confirming the border between the two states after Finnish independence. Finland gained the Arctic port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petsamo">Petsamo</a> but gave up its claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-karelia">East Karelia</a>, which remained Soviet despite the Finnish volunteer expeditions there. It governed Finnish–Soviet relations until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> of 1939.</p></div>
